# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What not to track.
#
# The rule to remember is not this list. It is one sentence:
#
#     IF IT HAS A VALUE IN IT, IT DOES NOT GO IN GIT.
#
# Lists go stale. That sentence does not.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ── Secrets. This is the important part of the file. ────────────────────────
#
# .env holds the key that decrypts every credential n8n has saved. Commit it
# once and it is in history permanently - deleting the file and committing
# again does NOT remove it, because every earlier commit still has it, and so
# does every copy of the repo.
#
# Track .env.example instead: the names, none of the values.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
credentials.json
service-account*.json
secrets/

# ── Data. Yours, and too big, and not source. ───────────────────────────────
postgres-data/
pgdata/
*.sql.gz
*.dump
*.sqlite
*.db

# ── Things a machine can rebuild ────────────────────────────────────────────
node_modules/
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
dist/
build/
.cache/

# ── Big binaries. Git is bad at these and they are not source. ──────────────
videos/
*.mp4
*.mov
*.wav
*.zip

# ── Machine noise ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*.log
